   - Once every issue you triaged has gone to a spec, `bmesh feedback drain --seq <their sequences, comma-separated>`, which deletes exactly those; the specs' `from_feedback` is the only record left. Then run `bmesh build <repo> NNN` for each quick fix: a family with feedback pending on that repo holds its builds, so the drain comes first. That mints a new family, and you watch its lead the same way, this step included; if a message that arrived after your list holds it, list and drain again. If you stop between list and drain, the family stays pending and `bmesh mesh-status` shows `feedback: N pending in <family>`.
   - Report one line per issue: `<issue>: fixed by NNN (started)`, `proposed NNN`, `already NNN`, `dropped: <why>` or `to the fixer`, then `drained N`.
   - **Release.** Last, when the drain started no quick fix, run `bmesh release <repo>` for each repo a lead of the family landed in that already has a release tag (`git -C <repo path> tag --list 'v*'` prints one), except bmesh's own repo while a fixer is live (a `<repo>-fix-<N>` node in `bmesh mesh-status` neither `done` nor `blocked`), which releases it once its queue is empty: the batch is done, so it gets its version once. In bmesh's own repo (the repo whose `go.mod` declares bmesh's module), write its upgrade notes first: `bmesh release <repo> --dry-run` names the tag and the specs landed since the last one (while specs with steps have no notes yet it refuses, naming those specs and the file to write); read the `## Upgrading` section of every spec landed since the last tag and distil them into the fewest true steps (a key one spec adds and another drops never appears; two moves of the same file become one; an ask whose default keeps today's behaviour stays an ask), written for any fleet; write them to `docs/upgrading/vX.Y.Z.toml` in the format the README's Upgrade notes section documents, commit it to `main` as `Upgrade notes vX.Y.Z` and push, then release. You have the final say over what goes in; the spec sections are your input, not the notes. When every section is empty or reads `nothing`, there is no file to write. `release` refuses (exit 3) when steps have no notes, or when the notes don't parse or name a key the binary doesn't know: fix the file and release again. It prints `released <repo> vX.Y.Z at <sha8> (NNN, ...)`; report that line. When quick fixes were started, their family's own pass through this step releases instead, so the batch still increments once. `nothing to release since ...` (only specs landed) and `already released ...` need nothing; a `held` or `main is red` refusal is reported to the operator with its line, never passed by hand. A repo with no release tag is not released from here: its first tag is the operator's (or a lead's, by hand).
